BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:In Crow's Field
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260511T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260511T203000
UID:bm-5071720260511@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nJoin us for the launch of\nIn Crow's Field\nby Jud
 ith Thompson. Meet the author\, mingle\, and get your book signed!\nJudith
  Thompson\nis an award-winning playwright\, director\, actor\, and a profe
 ssor of theatre studies at the University of Guelph. She has twice won the
  Governor General’s Award\, for the play\nWhite Biting Dog\n(1984) and t
 he collection of plays\nThe Other Side of the Dark\n(1989)\, and has been 
 recognized with many other awards including the Order of Canada\, the Walt
 er Carsen Performing Arts Award\, the Toronto Arts Award\, the Epilepsy On
 tario Award\, several Dora Mavor Moore Awards\, the Floyd S. Chalmers Awar
 d for the plays\nI Am Yours\n(1987) and\nLion in the Streets\n(1991)\, the
  Amnesty International Freedom of Expression Award\, the B’nai B’rith 
 Award\, and many others. Her plays have been produced in many countries an
 d translated into several languages. In 2008\, she was the first Canadian 
 to receive the Susan Smith Blackburn Prize. She has received honorary doct
 orates from Thorneloe University in Sudbury\, Ontario\, and Queen’s Univ
 ersity in Kingston\, Ontario. Born in Montréal\, she now lives in Toronto
 .\nIn Crow’s Field\nis her first novel.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening of Poetry: Sue Sinclair\, Puneet Dutt. and Phoebe Wang
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T203000
UID:bm-5071820260514@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nJoin us as Sue Sinclair\, Ronna Bloom\, and Puneet
  Dutt discuss poetry with host Jim Johnstone.\nMeet the authors\, mingle\,
  and get your book signed!\nSue Sinclair (she/her)\ngrew up in Newfoundlan
 d on the ancestral homelands of the Beothuk. She is the author of six prev
 ious collections of poetry\, including most recently\nAlmost Beauty: New a
 nd Selected Poems\n(Goose Lane Editions\, 2022)\, winner of New Brunswick
 ’s Fiddlehead Poetry Book Prize.\nHeaven’s Thieves\n(Brick Books\, 201
 6) won the Pat Lowther Award for the best book of poetry by a Canadian wom
 an. Sue teaches creative writing at the University of New Brunswick on Wə
 lastəkwey territory\, land of the “beautiful and bountiful river.”\nP
 uneet Dutt\nreceived her MA in English from Ryerson University. She is the
  author of the chapbook\nPTSD south beach\n(Grey Borders Books)\, which wa
 s a Finalist for the 2016 Breitling Chapbook Prize (Phantom Books). Her wo
 rk has been published in a number of journals and in\nImaginarium 4: The B
 est Canadian Speculative Writing\n. She was born in India and raised in Ne
 w Jersey. She now resides in Toronto with her husband\, where she is an ed
 itorial board member at Canthius and a creative writing workshop facilitat
 or with the Toronto Writers Collective. She can be found online at puneetd
 utt.com.\nPhoebe Wang\nis a writer and educator based in Toronto\, Canada\
 , and a first-generation Chinese-Canadian. Her debut collection of poetry\
 ,\nAdmission Requirements\nwas shortlisted for the Gerald Lampert Memorial
  Award\, the Pat Lowther Memorial Award\, and nominated for the Trillium B
 ook Award. She is a poetry editor with\nThe Fiddlehead Magazine\nand serve
 d as the 2021-2022 Writer-In-Residence at the University of New Brunswick.
 \nJim Johnstone\nis a poet\, editor\, and critic. His most recent books in
 clude a poetry collection\,\nThe King of Terrors\n(Coach House Books)\, as
  well as a book on micropress:\nWrite\, Print\, Fold\, and Staple\n(Gasper
 eau Press). Johnstone runs Anstruther Press with his wife\, Erica Smith\, 
 and curates the Anstruther Books imprint at Palimpsest Press\, where he pu
 blished\nThe Next Wave: An Anthology of 21st Century Canadian Poetry\nin 2
 018.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zak Jones in Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T203000
UID:bm-5161520260521@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nJoin us for a conversation with Zak Jones\, author
  of\nFancy Gap.\nMeet the author\, mingle\, and get your book signed!\nZak
  Jones\nis a writer\, literary scholar and US Army Veteran. A dual Canadia
 n-American citizen\, he grew up between rural North Carolina and Toronto. 
 His short story\, "So Much More to Say\," won the Writers’ Trust of Cana
 da’s 2023 Bronwen Wallace Award for Emerging Writers\, and his story\, "
 Love Handles\," won the 2023 Norma Epstein National Award. Zak holds an MA
  in English Literature & Creative Writing from the University of Toronto\,
  where he is currently a Ph.D. candidate studying veterans’ narratives i
 n American Literature.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Restaurant Kid
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T200000
UID:bm-5072020260526@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[Off-site]\nThe Toronto Public Library presents Rachel Phan at
  TPL's Yorkville Branch!\nRachel Phan\nis a Chinese Canadian author. A gra
 duate of Toronto Metropolitan University’s Master of Journalism program\
 , she and her work have been featured on CBC\, Huffpost\, the\nWall Street
  Journal\, the Washington Post\,\nthe\nToronto Star\,\nthe\nNational Post\
 , Chatelaine\,\nand\nMaclean’s\n. Her instant national bestselling book\
 ,\nRestaurant Kid: A Memoir of Family and Belonging\n(2025)\, was named on
 e of CBC Books’ Best Books of 2025 and longlisted for Canada Reads in 20
 26. She lives in Toronto\, ON.
LOCATION:22 Yorkville Ave\, TPL Yorkville Branch
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Threads\, Objects\, Storytelling
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T203000
UID:bm-5269820260528@shop.queenbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8100306/att.KeGn9
 4akg21xdOkMc3g_yoGvGtOQP3PmsKcqz28v_qQ.JPG?cb=1778174181
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nPlease join Alex and Diana for an evening of conve
 rsation. Both authors will read from their books\, discuss the inspiration
 s they have shared over the past decade\, and take questions from the audi
 ence before signing books. Alex will share a writing prompt and Diana will
  have vintage textiles on hand for those interested in starting a stitchin
 g project. Their hope is that guests will walk out into the night with a s
 eed of an idea or approach to a creative act (writing\, making\, activism)
  and a feeling of togetherness.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
